<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">August Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The August Revolution was the seizure of power across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> in August 1945, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Uprisings took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> on 19 August, Hue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> soon after, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> abdicated on 30 August. On 2 September <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, opening the conflict with returning French forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/august-revolution.jpg" alt="August Revolution" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vũ Năng An · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A1ch_m%E1%BA%A1ng_th%C3%A1ng_8_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The French ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 1884 until the Japanese coup of March 1945. In 1887 they created the Indochinese Union from Tonkin, Annam, Cochinchina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, adding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> in 1893. In September 1940, months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> capitulated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Japanese troops entered northern Vietnam, and until March 1945 the French kept their administration and colonial army in exchange for letting Japanese troops pass. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, created in 1941, was an anti-imperialist front led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> and rallied support across political lines around Vietnamese independence. The Empire of Vietnam, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, was led by the Nguyễn dynasty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-trong-kim">Trần Trọng Kim</a>'s government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 13 August 1945, on news that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was about to surrender, the Party's Central Committee and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> headquarters formed the National Uprising Committee, which issued Military Order No. 1 calling for a general uprising. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a> at Tân Trào on 16 August created the National Liberation Committee with Hồ Chí Minh as chairman. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-nam-quoc-dan-dang">Việt Nam Quốc Dân Đảng</a> had seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-giang-province">Hà Giang</a> on 13 August.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> surrendered on 15 August, People's Revolutionary Committees across the countryside took over administration, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> on the morning of 19 August. The Japanese army was largely passive, though the Viet Minh besieged the garrison at Thai Nguyen on 20 August. On 22 August Field Marshal Hisaichi Terauchi pledged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> that Japan would not intervene.</p><p>The Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> and his cabinet chose not to resist, so the transfer of power in major cities cost little blood. A telegram gave him twelve hours to abdicate, and he did so at Huế on 30 August. On 2 September Hồ Chí Minh declared the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The new state lacked international recognition, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> still recognised French sovereignty. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> the Allies had divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> at the 16th parallel, leaving the north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s China, whose 200,000 soldiers under Lu Han occupied it, and the south to the British. British forces arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 13 September with French troops.</p><p>At a rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 2 September the French fired on protesters, killing 47, and a general uprising followed on 23 September. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> also hunted down and executed leaders of rival militias, among them the Trotskyists Tạ Thu Thâu and Phan Văn Hùm. It repressed Trotskyists and non-communist nationalist parties, dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> into hostile camps and beginning three decades of conflict, and the anti-communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> was established in 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “August Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Japan’s surrender, the Viet Minh seized power in the <b>August Revolution</b>, and on 2 September 1945 Ho Chi Minh proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam in Hanoi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Japan’s surrender the Viet Minh seized power in the <b>August Revolution</b>, and on 30 August 1945 Bao Dai abdicated at Hue, handing the imperial seal and sword to the new government and ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After political purging and a corrupt, rigged election in 1906 veterans of the war of independence took up arms; Afro-Cubans, over-represented in the insurgent army as in the war of independence, saw in the <b>August Revolution</b> a hope of their rightful share in government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>August Revolution</b> of 1945 the Viet Minh, a front led by the Indochinese Communist Party, seized power across much of Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam</a></li><li><blockquote>After Japan's surrender, the Viet Minh launched the <b>August Revolution</b> and seized Hanoi.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-vietnam">Empire of Vietnam</a></li></ul></section>
